# `.` on a description. The site that answers is `rt_no_field`, not
# `rt_field_by_name`: a field name some record declares compiles to a getter,
# and a getter that matches nothing ends in that call. `(opaque d).nope`, with
# no record declaring `nope`, is a name error and never reaches the runtime —
# so reading the emitter says this site is unreachable and running a program
# says it is not.
# The check refuses a provable box here now (the errors corpus pins that
# sentence), so the box travels through a list, where nothing can prove it.
import "std/io"

type cell
  n

pub fn opaque v
  (push [] v)[1]!

d = io/write "a\n" >> io/write "b\n"

pub play = print "{(opaque d).n}"
